        ":question/id": "045c9a04-0733-55bf-8771-d8bc70944d9f",
        ":question/problem": "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{26}{3x}?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{26}{3}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{26}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{26}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=\\frac{26}{3}\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{26}{3x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln|x|+\\frac{26}{3}\\ln K \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      "role": "independently authored inventory",
      "relationship_to_source_templates": "Same parameterized reciprocal/linear numerator family as MA originals; this generated inventory does not establish observed difficulty."
