        ":question/id": "b39b559e-6dd3-51ab-936e-f0e9c4871293",
        ":question/problem": "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{15}{2x})}^{2}?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{15}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{15}{x}+\\frac{225}{4{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+15\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{225}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+15\\ln|x|+\\frac{225{x}^{-2+1}}{4(-2+1)}+C \\\\  & =x+15\\ln|x|-\\frac{225}{4x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=15\\ln K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $15\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{15}{2x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15\\ln|x|+15\\ln K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15(\\ln|x|+\\ln K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{225}{4x}+15\\ln(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"
